        <section>
          <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9"/>
          <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Assessment and Plan"/>
          <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
          <text>
            <paragraph>72yo F with AFib (off anticoagulation) presents with 20-minute episode of right-sided weakness and slurred speech, now resolved. Classic TIA. MRI shows no acute infarct but multiple old lacunar infarcts. Patient has been off warfarin for 3 months after subdural hematoma and falls. This is the consequence - predictable given CHADSVASc 5. Neurology consulted. DIFFICULT DECISION: 1) Restart anticoagulation - but patient remains at high bleeding risk (HAS-BLED score 5: age >65, uncontrolled HTN, labile INR history, prior bleed, CKD, alcohol), 2) Continue no anticoagulation - but stroke risk now demonstrated, 3) Reconsider LAAC device - but surgical risk high. Family and patient understand situation. After discussion, patient agrees to trial of apixaban 2.5mg BID (lower dose DOAC given age, weight, creatinine) rather than warfarin. Close monitoring.</paragraph>
          </text>
        </section>
